Premenstrual Syndrome (PMS)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Cyclical luteal phase symptoms, Affective lability/irritability, Resolution with onset of menses

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-042
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe emotional crisis involving extreme irritability, uncontrollable crying, and severe migraine just prior to expected menses.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a severe, acute psychological decompensation (crying uncontrollably, extreme affective lability) combined with a severe, debilitating migraine occurring consistently two days before her period. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to provide acute migraine relief, de-escalate the emotional crisis, and rule out a primary psychiatric emergency before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My head is throbbing so intensely I am vomiting, and I feel so overwhelmingly angry and depressed that I cannot stop crying.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms escalated rapidly over the past 48 hours, aligning with the late luteal phase of her menstrual cycle (menses expected in 2 days). The patient reports a predictable cyclical recurrence of these symptoms monthly. She describes severe affective lability, profound irritability, and a temporarily depressed mood that feels overwhelming. Concurrently, she is experiencing a severe exacerbation of her migraines, accompanied by subjective sensations of abdominal bloating, fluid retention, and pronounced breast tenderness (mastalgia). She notes generalized fatigue and insomnia over the last week. She emphatically states that these symptoms experience complete symptom resolution rapidly following the onset of menstrual flow.
Functional Status: Confined to a dark bedroom, functionally impaired by migraine and emotional distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: High level of acute distress; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 90 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 63 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely distressed, photophobic, tearful, and holding her head.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam is unremarkable for focal deficits; photophobia and phonophobia are prominent. Abdomen is soft, with mild diffuse bloating but no focal tenderness. Breasts are bilaterally tender to light palpation but without discrete masses. Mental Status Exam reveals a highly labile, irritable, and tearful affect, but insight is fully intact; she recognizes the cyclical hormonal trigger. No suicidal ideation.
Environmental Observations: Room is completely dark and quiet.
Medication Reconciliation: Took ibuprofen 400mg with no relief for the migraine.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Premenstrual Syndrome (PMS) / Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder (PMDD) flare with acute menstrual migraine - Status: Acute exacerbation
Differentials:

Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out by the strict, predictable cyclical nature of the symptoms restricted purely to the luteal phase, with total resolution after menses.

Primary Migraine - The migraine is present, but it is a secondary symptom of the broader, hormonally driven PMS/PMDD complex.

Hypothyroidism - Unlikely given the rapid cyclical fluctuation of the profound symptoms, though baseline thyroid testing is appropriate later.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, incapacitating flare of Premenstrual Syndrome (likely PMDD given the severity of the affective symptoms), complicated by a severe menstrual migraine. The objective clinical presentation confirms the diagnostic tetrad of severe luteal phase symptoms, profound affective lability, and an intact realization that this will resolve with menses. The severity of the migraine causing vomiting, combined with the extreme emotional distress, required immediate after-hours intervention to administer parenteral migraine therapy (triptan/antiemetic) and provide acute psychological grounding. Delayed assessment would have resulted in an unnecessary emergency department presentation for unmanageable pain and psychiatric distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered subcutaneous sumatriptan 6mg for acute migraine abortive therapy, and intramuscular metoclopramide 10mg for nausea. Provided acute psychological reassurance, validating the profound physical and chemical reality of PMDD. Instructed the patient to begin a prospective daily symptom diary for two consecutive cycles to formally document the luteal phase restriction for her GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately reduce the sensory overload worsening your migraine and mood, remain in this completely dark, quiet room with a cold pack over your eyes for the next hour; experiencing the rapid reduction in throbbing will reinforce the need for immediate sensory withdrawal. To help your GP formally diagnose this severe syndrome, diligently track your mood and pain on a calendar every single day; seeing the undeniable pattern will validate your experience and unlock specific medical treatments.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in two weeks to discuss continuous oral contraceptives or luteal-phase SSRIs for long-term PMDD management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if you develop the worst headache of your life suddenly (thunderclap), if you experience weakness or numbness on one side of your body, or if you begin to feel actively suicidal.

